The strongest surviving capability is adhesion that preserves a stored order while its contact spacings changeI have sustained the retained-order branch and given it a more demanding function: a surface should recognize the same ordered pattern after substantial redistribution of the distances between its sites, and retain a useful normal adhesion response while individual contacts remain able to release.A common physical model now supports that operation conditionally. It includes finite contact geometry, competing and mismatched contacts, sliding entropy, unwanted depletion contacts at stops, and the mechanical path by which a support pulls a seated partner out.The conventional comparison materially limits the result. A conventional linkage can reproduce the calculated response when given enough separately allocated travel. In the comparison below, the rail uses 12 μm of shared slack; the best numerical conventional design found needs about 29.1 μm of separately allocated travel to match its combined adhesion-work and discrimination targets. That is a distinction in a specified mechanical resource. Its value as a material depends on how economically each construction can supply that resource.There is also a consequential failure: the four-contact example is thermally too weak to act as a robust force selector on its own, despite having separated correct-pattern and wrong-pattern force curves.1. A physical contact that accommodates registration and releaseI replaced the buried, tightly registered contact with an open spherical cup and a spherical key. Each cup sits on a carriage retained on a single capped guide. The carriages cannot pass one another, but their axial separations can change. The external keys sit on a facing support, with bounded transverse travel.This separates four requirements in an actual mechanical construction:Retention: captivity on the capped guide preserves the sequence of carriage types.Sliding: the carriages redistribute the available axial space.Registration: a sphere seats against a complementary spherical cavity; sphere orientation does not have to be selected.Release: the sphere can withdraw through the open mouth while other contacts remain seated.Each row uses one continuous guide. External bonds do not become part of the sliding path, so this version does not require a carriage to traverse an exchanging network junction.The chemical ingredient has primary experimental support. Sacanna and colleagues demonstrated reversible, shape-selective colloidal binding through depletion attraction between spherical keys and particles with spherical cavities. Their results also establish an important correction: an oversized key can bind at a cavity rim. Geometric mismatch cannot simply be represented as a zero binding constant. irvinelab.uchicago.eduThe calculation uses a specified hemispherical shell rather than treating those experimental particles as exact copies of the proposed geometry. Its potential isU={+∞,hard overlap,−ΠVoverlap,otherwise,U=
